# Security headers for every file Cloudflare serves straight from static
# assets: JS, CSS, images and any data shards.
#
# Page responses never come through here — the Worker renders those and
# next.config.ts sets their headers, including the real page CSP. Without this
# file every static asset answers with none of them, which an SEO crawl reports
# as dozens of URLs missing security headers.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'unsafe-inline'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The ad frame, and the one place on this site where script-src is open.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN next.config.ts. Adsterra rotates its delivery
# domains on purpose to stay ahead of blocklists, so its Native Banner cannot
# run under a named allow-list. A CSP applies per document, so framing the unit
# from /ads/native.html confines the loose policy it needs to this one static
# file, which holds no data of any kind.
#
# next.config.ts has since had to open script-src for the Social Bar, which
# docks itself to the viewport and cannot be framed. That does not make this
# block redundant: this unit still runs somewhere with no result card in it,
# and if the site policy is ever tightened again the Native Banner keeps
# working rather than silently going blank.
#
# THE TWO `!` LINES ARE LOAD-BEARING, and this was learned the hard way.
#
# Matching rules here are COMBINED, not overridden. Declaring this block after
# /* sent both policies, and a browser given two Content-Security-Policy
# headers enforces the INTERSECTION of them — so `default-src 'none'` from the
# catch-all still refused every script, and the two X-Frame-Options values
# (DENY and SAMEORIGIN) resolved to the stricter one. The frame served 200 and
# rendered nothing, which looks exactly like a no-fill.
#
# `!` removes the inherited header first; the value below then stands alone.
#
# frame-ancestors 'self' and X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN together mean only
# this site can frame it — an ad page that anyone could embed is an open
# redirect for someone else's traffic, billed to this publisher account.
#
# NEVER widen this to cover more than /ads/*, and never put anything under
# /ads/ that reads a query string or renders site data.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/ads/*
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  ! X-Frame-Options
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; script-src 'unsafe-inline' https:; connect-src https:; img-src https: data: blob:; style-src 'unsafe-inline' https:; font-src https: data:; frame-src https:; media-src https:; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self'
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
